PASS  A.2: the client's parameters parse
PASS  A.2: initial_max_data is 2^62 - 1
PASS  A.2: each stream's credit is 65535
PASS  A.2: 16 streams each way
PASS  A.2: a 30 s idle timeout
PASS  A.2: initial_source_connection_id is its SCID
PASS  A.2: what it did not send keeps its default
PASS  parameters at their defaults encode to nothing
PASS  a server's full set encodes in ID order, each as ID, length and value
PASS  and parses back as a server's, every field as it was
PASS  unknown and reserved parameters are skipped
PASS  an ID cut inside its varint is refused
PASS  a length cut inside its varint is refused
PASS  a value running past the bytes is refused
PASS  a parameter sent twice is refused
PASS  original_destination_connection_id from a client is refused
PASS  a stateless reset token that is not 16 bytes is refused
PASS  a connection ID of 21 bytes is refused
PASS  disable_active_migration with a value is refused
PASS  a varint-valued parameter with a byte after its varint is refused
PASS  an empty varint-valued parameter is refused
PASS  max_udp_payload_size below 1200 is refused
PASS  ack_delay_exponent above 20 is refused
PASS  max_ack_delay of 2^14 is refused
PASS  active_connection_id_limit below 2 is refused
PASS  a stream count past 2^60 is refused
PASS  the boundary values are accepted
PASS  nothing received writes no ACK
PASS  0, 1 and 2 are new
PASS  and make one range
PASS  2 again is a duplicate
PASS  as is a packet number that is not one
PASS  an ack-eliciting packet makes an ACK due
PASS  5 starts a second range above
PASS  4 joins it from below
PASS  3 closes the hole and merges them
PASS  7 and 9 make two more
PASS  8 merges those two
PASS  6 merges everything
PASS  contains says what was received
PASS  pushAck writes the ranges
PASS  as largest 12, delay 3, one more range: [12, 12] then [0, 9]
PASS  and the ACK is no longer due
PASS  an index outside the ranges answers -1
PASS  and set outside them changes nothing
PASS  forty disjoint packets keep QUIC_ACK_MAX_RANGES ranges
PASS  and raise the floor over the ones dropped
PASS  a packet number at or below the floor reads as received
PASS  one above it is still new
PASS  a new lowest range, with every slot taken, is itself dropped
PASS  the first local ID is sequence 0
PASS  the next sequence 1
PASS  both are active and owned
PASS  sequence 1 still has to be announced; sequence 0 never does
PASS  and once it is, none is left
PASS  retiring a sequence number never issued is PROTOCOL_VIOLATION
PASS  so is a negative one
PASS  so is retiring the ID the packet was sent to (RFC 9000 §19.16)
PASS  retiring another is fine
PASS  and it is no longer owned
PASS  retiring it twice is fine too
PASS  before the peer gave an ID there is none to send to
PASS  the peer's first SCID is sequence 0
PASS  a NEW_CONNECTION_ID adds sequence 1
PASS  the same frame again is fine
PASS  sequence 1 with another ID is PROTOCOL_VIOLATION
PASS  sequence 1 with another token is too
PASS  an ID under a second sequence number is too
PASS  two are active, and the oldest is the one sent to
PASS  a third active ID passes the limit of 2: CONNECTION_ID_LIMIT_ERROR
PASS  Retire Prior To 2 retires both older IDs
PASS  so only the new one is active and sent to
PASS  and 0 then 1 are queued to retire
PASS  an ID that arrives already below Retire Prior To is retired at once
PASS  and queued
PASS  four queued retirements, twice the limit, are allowed
PASS  a fifth is CONNECTION_ID_LIMIT_ERROR (RFC 9000 §5.1.2)
PASS  and the refused frame leaves the table within its bounds
PASS  with the ID sent to unchanged
PASS  prune leaves it as it was
